The distance from $2$ is at most $4$.
$\begin{aligned}-4&\le x-2\le4\\-2&\le x\le6\end{aligned}$
The solution is $[-2,6]$, including both endpoints.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-3,0)--(7.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.5)--(0,6.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->] (-3,5)--(2,0)--(7,5);
\draw[thick,curveRed,<->] (-3,4)--(7,4);
\path (4.5,2) node[right,text=curveBlue] {$y=|x-2|$};
\path (6,4.6) node[text=curveRed] {$y=4$};
\foreach \v in {-2,2,6}{\draw[black] (\v,-0.1)--(\v,0.1);\path (\v,-0.2) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[black] (-0.1,4)--(0.1,4);
\path (-0.2,4) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$4$};
\draw[dashed,black] (-2,0)--(-2,4) (6,0)--(6,4);
\fill[black] (-2,4) circle (1.4pt) (6,4) circle (1.4pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue graph is on or below the red line for $-2\le x\le6$.

The distance from $2$ must exceed $3$, so use two alternatives.
$\begin{aligned}x-2&<-3\\x&<-1\end{aligned}$
or
$\begin{aligned}x-2&>3\\x&>5\end{aligned}$
The solution is $(-\infty,-1)\cup(5,\infty)$. Neither boundary is included.
